Hi,

in your code you do predictions onto the mesh vertices. In that case,
you can use inla.mesh.project() to interpolate that to a lattice, and
use image() and similar functions to plot it.

See page 14 and 20 of the "Read this first" tutorial here:
  http://www.r-inla.org/examples/tutorials

In your code, the "pred" stack computes the effects of the spde&intercept only:

stack.pred =
   inla.stack(data=list(rain=NA),
               A=list(A.pred),
               effects=
                 list(c(field.indices,
                        list(Intercept=1))),
               tag="pred")

 You could make a separate prediction stack for the covariates. The
best way to do this depends on the details of what you want to
compute/plot.
For example, to predict the combined effect of covariates but
excluding intercept and spde,

stack.covpred =
    inla.stack(data=list(rain=NA),
               A=list(1),
               effects=
                 list(list(Elevation=val.elevation)),
               tag="covpred")

Finn

